{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For $0<\alpha<\beta$, abbreviate $D_\alpha=D(u_\alpha)$ and $J_\alpha=J(u_\alpha)$. Optimality of the two <variational regularization> solutions gives
$$
D_\alpha+\alpha J_\alpha\leq D_\beta+\alpha J_\beta,\qquad
D_\beta+\beta J_\beta\leq D_\alpha+\beta J_\alpha.
$$
Adding shows $(\beta-\alpha)(J_\beta-J_\alpha)\leq0$, so $J_\beta\leq J_\alpha$. Rearranging each comparison then gives the <monotonicity of data fidelity and regularization penalty>
$$
\boxed{0\leq\alpha(J_\alpha-J_\beta)
\leq D_\beta-D_\alpha
\leq\beta(J_\alpha-J_\beta).}
$$
Hence **the regulariser value is non-increasing and the data misfit is non-decreasing with the parameter**. These inequalities hold for any choices of minimizers at the two distinct parameters, including when uniqueness fails.

= d
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Comparison with zero and nonnegativity of the two terms gives
$$
0\leq D(u_\alpha)+\alpha J(u_\alpha)
\leq D(0)+\alpha J(0)=\frac12\|f\|^2.
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{0\leq J(u_\alpha)\leq\frac{\|f\|^2}{2\alpha},\qquad
J(u_\alpha)\longrightarrow0\quad(\alpha\to\infty).}
$$
For a concrete <variational regularization> example, take a <Hilbert space> and $J(u)=\|u\|^2$. The bound forces $u_\alpha\to0$ in <norm>. Since $K$ is a <bounded linear operator>, $Ku_\alpha\to0$, and continuity of the squared <norm> gives
$$
\boxed{D(u_\alpha)\longrightarrow\tfrac12\|f\|^2.}
$$
The same conclusion is not forced by $J(u_\alpha)\to0$ for a regulariser with a nontrivial zero set.

= e
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

The small-parameter limit depends on <data compatibility with the regularizer domain>. Set
$$
m=\inf_{v\in\operatorname{dom}J}D(v),\qquad 0\leq m\leq D(0).
$$
For every such $v$, optimality gives
$$
m\leq D(u_\alpha)\leq\Phi_{\alpha,f}(u_\alpha)
\leq D(v)+\alpha J(v).
$$
Taking the upper limit as $\alpha\downarrow0$ and then the <infimum> over $v$ proves $\Phi_{\alpha,f}(u_\alpha)\to m$. Its two nonnegative contributions above $m$ must vanish, yielding the general result
$$
\boxed{D(u_\alpha)\longrightarrow m,\qquad
\alpha J(u_\alpha)\longrightarrow0.}
$$
In particular, the requested zero-misfit limit holds when $f\in\overline{K(\operatorname{dom}J)}$. A sufficient condition is an exact solution $v$ with $Kv=f$ and $J(v)<\infty$; then
$$
0\leq D(u_\alpha)\leq\alpha J(v),\qquad
0\leq\alpha J(u_\alpha)\leq\alpha J(v)\longrightarrow0.
$$
**Membership of $f$ in $\mathcal R(K)$ alone is insufficient.** Take $\mathcal U=\mathcal V=\mathbb R$, $K=I$, $f=1$, and $J$ the <indicator functional of a constraint set> $\{0\}$. All positive-parameter objectives are <proper extended-real functions> with <coercivity> and <sequential lower semicontinuity>, with the unique minimizer $u_\alpha=0$, yet $D(u_\alpha)=1/2$ for every $\alpha$. Thus the first printed limit needs compatibility with the regulariser's <effective domain>; the second limit remains true under the given assumptions.

= 2
{parent=3}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For a <functional> $E$ on a real <Banach space>, its <convex conjugate> is defined on the <dual space> by
$$
\boxed{E^*(p)=\sup_{u\in\mathcal U}\{\langle p,u\rangle-E(u)\},\qquad p\in\mathcal U^*.}
$$
Here $\langle p,u\rangle$ is the <dual pairing>. For complex spaces, use its real part, equivalently regard the space as real for <convex analysis>. The <convex conjugate> is a <convex function>, being the supremum of <affine functions> of $p$, even when $E$ itself is not <convex>.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $G=E\mathbin\square F$. To prove <convexity>, take $u_1,u_2$ with finite $G$ values, $0<t<1$, and $\varepsilon>0$. By the definition of <infimum>, choose $v_i$ with
$$
E(v_i)+F(u_i-v_i)\leq G(u_i)+\varepsilon.
$$
Put $u_t=tu_1+(1-t)u_2$ and $v_t=tv_1+(1-t)v_2$. <Convexity> of $E$ and $F$ gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
G(u_t)&\leq E(v_t)+F(u_t-v_t)\\
&\leq t[E(v_1)+F(u_1-v_1)]
+(1-t)[E(v_2)+F(u_2-v_2)]\\
&\leq tG(u_1)+(1-t)G(u_2)+\varepsilon.
\end{aligned}
$$
Let $\varepsilon\downarrow0$. If either endpoint value is infinite, the convexity inequality is automatic, and $t=0,1$ gives equality. Thus **the <infimal convolution> of two <convex functions> is <convex>** under the stated properness assumption. The proof uses approximate minimizing splits, so no attainment of the inner <infimum> is assumed.

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For $p\in\mathcal U^*$, the definition of the <convex conjugate> gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
(E\mathbin\square F)^*(p)
&=\sup_u\sup_v\{\langle p,u\rangle-E(v)-F(u-v)\}\\
&=\sup_{v,w}\{\langle p,v\rangle-E(v)+\langle p,w\rangle-F(w)\}\\
&=E^*(p)+F^*(p),
\end{aligned}
$$
where the substitution $w=u-v$ is a bijection of the independent pairs. Therefore
$$
\boxed{(E\mathbin\square F)^*=E^*+F^*.}
$$
This <infimal convolution> identity does not need <convexity> of $E,F$ or attainment of the inner <infimum>. The assumptions ensure that both functions have nonempty <effective domains>; their conjugates never take $-\infty$, so the separated sum is well defined, allowing $+\infty$.

= d
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Write $\iota_C$ for the <indicator functional of a constraint set> $C$ and $q(u)=\|u\|^2/2$. Then
$$
d_C=\iota_C\mathbin\square q.
$$
Both terms are <convex>, hence their <infimal convolution> is the <convex> <squared distance to a convex set>. The <closest point theorem in a Hilbert space> supplies the unique <metric projection onto a closed convex set> $P_Cu$ and gives $d_C(u)=\|u-P_Cu\|^2/2$.

Identify the <Hilbert space> with its <dual space> using the <Riesz representation theorem>. Completing the square yields $q^*(p)=\|p\|^2/2$, while $\iota_C^*(p)=\sup_{v\in C}\langle p,v\rangle=\sigma_C(p)$ is the <support function>. The <conjugate of the squared distance to a convex set> is therefore
$$
\boxed{d_C^*(p)=\frac12\|p\|^2+\sigma_C(p).}
$$
For the closed unit ball, the <metric projection onto a closed convex set> is $P_Cu=u$ if $\|u\|\leq1$, and $P_Cu=u/\|u\|$ otherwise. Therefore
$$
\boxed{d_C(u)=\frac12\bigl(\max\{\|u\|-1,0\}\bigr)^2,\qquad
d_C^*(p)=\frac12\|p\|^2+\|p\|.}
$$
The last equality uses the <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality> to compute the unit ball's <support function>, attained in the direction of $p$ when $p\neq0$.
